 and so the base layer of this  is sending all your logs off of the  cluster to s3e Marv we'll give you some  of this for free HDFS and yarn it'll  take care of for any of the other things  that you're working with pigs bark  Luigi's on here too you have to get all  the application logs yourself and make  sure that they are they arrive where you  need them but this is super essential  because nothing is going to be around in  the morning when you go to look for it  logs are nice but sometimes it's very  difficult in a distributed system to  piece together exactly what happened so  we have some visualizations that we run  on top of that that help lip stick shows  us exactly what happened in any job it  keeps all the counters it's sort of the  visualization that I showed earlier the  spark history server is a good version  of this for spark you can actually  submit all of your data there we don't  have that up and running yet we're  actually working on that now and then we  have a task flow visualization for Luigi  which shows us which tasks ran when and  which ones failed so that helps you sort  of orient yourself into the logs so  you're not just surfing through text and  finally it's very very important to  preserve your monitoring data  historically when you have machines that  are going up and down all the time you  need to be able to look back  like say I want just the anomaly  detection cluster and I want to see what  it was doing for the last week even when  that hardware is going something else  that's really important is you need to  make your job layer actually able to  handle failure for us the answer for  that is Luigi Luigi is a workflow tool  that Spotify open-sourced a little bit  ago it's quite nice for this the thing  that Luigi does is allow you to take a  very long complicated pipeline of tasks  that you want to do and split them up  into small individual tasks each of  those have a very simple semantics for  how to make the tasks clean up after  itself if it fails and how to make sure  that it can restart well also each task  knows how to detect whether it's already  run and not do anything if that's  happened so this is a very simple  example just two tasks in a pipeline a  and B imagine that we have tasks a and  then B depends on it  so if we run our pipeline and task a  succeeds it's good but imagine that task  B is running and the cluster that's  running on gets reclaimed for spot  instances so the task fails what Luigi's  gonna do is actually clean up all of the  partial data that may have been written  by that task and then we can just rerun  the pipeline from the start with no  additional parameters and it's gonna  launch the cluster run it again and the  job will succeed this is really nice  because it means that no human has to  get involved when there's a failure the  property that does this is called  idempotence and it just means that you  can just rerun jobs until they succeed  or until you timeout so in practice what  this does is make small failures that  happen all the time and our pipelines  not have to elevate to the level of a  human unless they're not resolving  themselves so to recap we talked about  the three different layers that we have  in the platform the the cloud storage  where we keep all of our data in a way  that's flexible doesn't require a lot of  management and lets us point mini  clusters at it the Elastic Compute where  we're running mini clusters throughout  the day using spot instances to make  them very cheap and allowing our data  scientists and engineers as much  capacity as they need and finally the  tooling layer around the edges which  helps us deal with this level of  dynamism and  be able to manage it so if you're just  thinking about how to do your own cloud  Big Data Platform or you have one that  you want to sort of move forward  have a couple recommendations for what  you might look into if you can and  you're in the cloud you should  definitely use s3 for your permanent  data and not HDFS this is actually  applicable to many other clouds as well  cloud storage is becoming a fairly  commoditize so Azure and Google cloud  have very good options for this as well  if you're in Amazon  don't start building for me from ec2  start from elastic MapReduce if you can  it's going to give you a lot out of the  box and eventually if you need to  rebuild those things you can but it's a  great place to start even better if if  you can look into using one of the  platform as-a-service that are available  Netflix has an open source one called  genie they're doing quite a bit of work  on this to move it from v2 to v3 right  now but Cubo and data breaks also have  very solid platforms data bricks is much  more focused on spark and Cube always is  pretty wide-ranging we found that  tagging is actually a really really good  way to make sure that all of these  things that you'll use to go up and down  or able to be tracked across time and  that your users don't have to think too  hard about which hardware that they need  to work on and finally make sure that  you get a workflow tool that has failure  baked in Luigi is a really good one for  this airflow is also really nice either  of those are going to work very well for  you so that's my talk thanks a lot  and if you like me are very excited  about these things and want to work on  spark and Hadoop and Kafka and all this  stuff with us hit me up on Twitter or  send me an email or hit our jobs board  we're always hiring thank you  [Applause]

